import{_ as a,o as i,c as r,a2 as t}from"./chunks/framework.AuRYXM_l.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"Hardware Flatform Feasibility","description":"Hardware Flatform Feasibility","frontmatter":{"title":"Hardware Flatform Feasibility","description":"Hardware Flatform Feasibility","source":"docs/archive/source-material/Hardware Flatform Feasibility.md","category":"Archive","sourceUpdated":"2026-10-08T06:33:54+08:00","outline":[2,3]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"content/docs/archive/source-material/hardware-flatform-feasibility.md","filePath":"content/docs/archive/source-material/hardware-flatform-feasibility.md"}'),o={name:"content/docs/archive/source-material/hardware-flatform-feasibility.md"};function s(n,e,l,d,p,c){return i(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[t('<div><h1 id="document-title">Hardware Flatform Feasibility<a class="header-anchor" href="#document-title" aria-label="Link to title">​</a></h1><p>Hardware Flatform Feasibility</p><p>Group # 13</p><p>BSIT -GD - J4S</p><p>Group Members: Luis M. Cabrido Iii</p><p>Sherwin P. Limosnero</p><p>Sean Matthew Lee Narvaez Christian Jude N. Villaber</p><p>PROFESSOR: Grache Elizal C. Greta</p><p>P a g e | 1</p><p>University of Perpetual Help System Laguna - JONELTA</p><p>College of Computer Studies</p><p>Part I. Review of Problem Statement Limited Access to Age-Appropriate Web3 Education for Young Learners Young learners have limited access to age-appropriate and engaging educational materials about Web3 and blockchain technology. Existing resources are often too technical and difficult for children to understand, while limited internet access can also prevent students from consistently accessing online educational resources. Part II. Target Users (Group Work) Identify the intended users of your proposed project. Examples: ● Elementary students ● Senior high school students ● College students ● Teachers ● Parents ● Office employees ● Small business owners ● Senior citizens Answer the following:</p><ol><li>Who are your target users? Our primary target users are Kindergarten to Elementary students, approximately 4–12 years old, who are interested in learning about technology and basic Web3 and blockchain concepts.</li><li>Why did you choose these users? We chose young learners because there are limited educational resources that introduce Web3 and blockchain concepts in a simple, engaging, and age-appropriate way. Introducing these concepts at an early age can help develop digital literacy, critical thinking, and awareness of emerging technologies.</li><li>What device do they most commonly use? Our target users are expected to commonly use Android smartphones and tablets, either personally or through devices provided by their parents, guardians, or schools. Android was selected because it is widely available across different price ranges and can support applications designed for low- to mid-range devices.</li></ol><p>P a g e | 2</p><p>University of Perpetual Help System Laguna - JONELTA</p><p>College of Computer Studies Part III. Platform Selection (Group Work) Choose the platform where your application or game will primarily run. Select only one. Android Windows macOS Web Browser iOS Console Virtual Reality (VR) Other Answer the following:</p><ol><li>Why is this platform the most appropriate for your project? Android is the most appropriate primary platform because the project is intended for young learners who can access educational content through smartphones and tablets. Android devices are available across a wide range of hardware and price levels, allowing the project to target users who may not have access to high-end computers. The 2D design of the game also allows it to run efficiently on relatively low-specification devices.</li><li>Is your target platform easily accessible to your intended users? Explain your answer. Yes. Android devices are widely available and are commonly used by families and schools for educational activities. The project will also be designed with relatively low hardware requirements so that learners using entry-level or mid-range Android devices can still access the game.</li></ol><p>Part IV. Hardware Requirement Analysis (Group Work) Hardware Component</p><p>Minimum Requirement</p><p>Recommended Requirement</p><p>Why is this appropriate for your project?</p><p>CPU Quad-core 2.0 GHz or equivalent</p><p>Octa-core 2.0 GHz or better</p><p>Supports 3D game logic, animations, educational activities, and background processes.</p><p>P a g e | 3</p><p>University of Perpetual Help System Laguna - JONELTA</p><p>College of Computer Studies</p><p>RAM 4 GB 6 GB or more 3 GB can support basic 3D gameplay, while 4–6 GB provides smoother performance. Storage 2 GB free space 4 GB or more Provides space for 3D models, textures, audio, animations, lessons, and offline content.</p><p>GPU Adreno 506 / Mali-G57 or equivalent</p><p>Adreno 610 / Mali-G68 or better</p><p>Provides sufficient graphics processing for 3D environments, models, textures, lighting, and animations.</p><p>Internet Connection</p><p>Not Required Not Required The game is designed to be fully playable offline, allowing learners to access the educational content without an internet connection.</p><p>When completing this table, consider the following: ● Will your application require internet access? ● Will it use high-quality graphics? ● Will it process large amounts of data? ● Will it be used on low-end or high-end devices? Do not simply list hardware specifications. Explain why they are appropriate for your proposed project.</p><p>The proposed hardware requirements are designed to support the project&#39;s 3D educational gameplay while remaining accessible to users with entry-level to mid-range Android devices. The game will primarily use 3D environments, character models, animations, interactive objects, educational activities, and visual effects, which require more processing power than a 2D game. The hardware requirements are therefore set to provide smooth and responsive gameplay while keeping the game optimized for mobile devices. Since the game will be fully playable offline, users will not need a constant internet connection to access the core learning content and gameplay.</p><p>P a g e | 4</p><p>University of Perpetual Help System Laguna - JONELTA</p><p>College of Computer Studies Part V. Hardware Feasibility (Group Work) Answer the following questions.</p><ol><li>Can your target users realistically meet these hardware requirements? Explain your answer. Yes. The requirements are designed to be achievable by many users because the game does not require high-end hardware. A basic Android smartphone or tablet with around 2 GB of RAM, a quad-core processor, sufficient storage, and a basic mobile GPU should be capable of running the game. This makes the project more accessible to young learners who may use devices shared with their parents, guardians, or schools.</li><li>What problems might users experience if their devices do not meet the minimum hardware requirements? Users with devices below the minimum requirements may experience slow loading times, lag, low frame rates, delayed responses, application crashes, or difficulty installing the game. Devices with insufficient storage may also be unable to download the application or additional offline educational content. Older devices may have difficulty running newer versions of the application or displaying some game features properly. To minimize these problems, the game will be optimized for lower-end devices and will avoid unnecessarily demanding graphics and effects.</li><li>Would you modify your project after learning about computer hardware? Yes. We would modify and optimize the project to make it accessible to as many target users as possible. Instead of using high-end 3D graphics, the project will focus on 2D graphics and lightweight game mechanics. We would also optimize images, audio, animations, and educational content to reduce storage and memory usage. The offline mode will also be designed so that users can access essential learning materials without requiring a continuous internet connection.</li></ol><p>Part VI. Reflection (Individual Response) Write a reflection and answer the following guide questions: ● What did you learn about computer hardware as a software developer? I learned that hardware plays an important role in software development because it affects how well an application performs. Understanding the CPU, RAM, storage, and</p><p>P a g e | 5</p><p>University of Perpetual Help System Laguna - JONELTA</p><p>College of Computer Studies</p><p>GPU helps developers create software that can run smoothly on the devices used by their target users. ● Why is hardware analysis important before starting software development? Hardware analysis is important because it helps developers determine the appropriate system requirements and avoid creating software that is too demanding for users&#39; devices. It also helps improve performance, accessibility, and the overall user experience. ● How will today&#39;s lesson help your group in developing your capstone project? This lesson will help our group optimize BlockPlay for Android devices by considering the hardware capabilities of our target users. We can design a 3D educational game that runs smoothly on low-end to mid-range devices while maintaining good graphics, performance, and offline functionality. This will make the game more accessible to young learners.</p></div>',1)])])}const h=a(o,[["render",s]]);export{m as __pageData,h as default};
